# Client-side (safe to ship in the bundle).
# Copy this file to .env and fill these in. Both are publishable by design:
# the anon key is useless without RLS passing, and every catalog policy is
# `to authenticated`.
#
# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> API
#
# The publishable key is the one labelled "publishable" and starting
# sb_publishable_... It replaced the older "anon key" (a JWT starting eyJ...);
# either still works and either variable name is accepted.
#
# NOT the secret key (sb_secret_... / service_role). That bypasses RLS and
# would be shipped inside the app bundle.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTHING ELSE BELONGS IN THIS FILE.
#
# The Google Maps key and the Anthropic key are server-side only. They are
# never read by the app, never committed, and have no entry here on purpose --
# an empty KEY= line is an invitation to fill it in.
#
# They live as Supabase Edge Function secrets:
#
#   supabase secrets set GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=...
#   supabase secrets set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...
#
# Why it matters: any variable prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ is inlined into the
# JavaScript bundle, where anyone who downloads the app can read it. For the
# Maps key that is a directly billable credential; see docs/measurements.md
# for the SKU prices.
#
# The app never needs a Google key. It calls places-proxy, which holds the
# key, enforces the per-user quota and returns only request-scoped values.
#
# scripts/check-no-google-persistence.py fails the build if either name
# appears anywhere under src/.
